--- a/DIVERGENCE.md
+++ b/DIVERGENCE.md
@@ -745,6 +745,87 @@ Registered as `pusher-through-child` and `rule-history-bounded-pusher`,
 with `pusher-through-child-control` as their control: `parent.child.name`,
 the same pusher read directly, where the three ports agree.
 
+### A fixed token another name has taken the source of
+
+**Deferred, not deliberate** — a limit of how Go holds its fixed tokens,
+recorded here because a merge reaches it as well as a single instance.
+
+TypeScript holds the fixed tokens as an ordered name → source table, its
+option tree's `fixed.token`, and `configure()` builds the lexer's source
+→ token map from it front to back. Where two names claim one source the
+later one lexes it, and the earlier stays in the table, lexing nothing,
+until the later one lets the source go. Rust holds the same table and its
+lexer takes the last of equal matches. Go holds only the source → token
+map: binding a name to a source another name holds overwrites that
+entry, the most recent binding wins, and the name that lost it keeps
+nothing.
+
+| options | `[` lexes as, TypeScript | Go | Rust |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `fixed.token: {"#CA": "["}` | `#CA` | `#CA` | `#CA` |
+| `fixed.token: {"#OB": "["}` | `#OS` | **`#OB`** | `#OS` |
+
+`#OS` is later than `#OB` in the defaults' order, so TypeScript keeps
+`#OB`'s binding behind it; Go's binding of `#OB` overwrote `#OS`'s.
+
+A merge carries the split on. All three ports now merge a removed fixed
+token as TypeScript does: a removal does not survive, the merged instance
+puts back every default neither side holds, and where a default put back
+and a binding a side holds claim one source, the later in the defaults'
+order lexes it. TypeScript and Rust keep the binding that lost; Go cannot:
+
+| sides | then | TypeScript | Go |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| A `{"#OS": null, "#OB": "["}`, B `{"#OS": null}` | `[` in A ⊕ B | `#OS` | `#OS` |
+| same | (A ⊕ B) ⊕ C `{"#OB": "("}` | refused: conflicting option values at `fixed.token.#OB` | **merges; `(` lexes as `#OB`** |
+| same | (A ⊕ B) ⊕ a default instance | refused: fixed tokens `#OB` and `#OS` both claim source `"["` | **merges** |
+| same | A ⊕ B, then `fixed.token: {"#OS": null}`; `[` | `#OB` | **`#TX`** |
+| same | A ⊕ B, then a grammar `clear`; bindings left | `#OB` on `[` | **none** |
+| A `{"#OB": "["}`, B `{"#OS": null}` | A ⊕ B | refused: fixed tokens `#OB` and `#OS` both claim source `"["` | **merges; `[` lexes as `#OS`** |
+
+The same limit puts the two names of that refusal in the other order when
+a side removed a default name and bound it again: TypeScript's tree moves
+the name to its end, and Go, which keeps no order, reads it in the
+defaults' place. Only the message differs; both refuse.
+
+Repair direction: **Go changes**, holding its fixed tokens as TypeScript
+does, as an ordered name → source table the source map is built from, so
+a binding can be shadowed and later freed. That reaches every door that
+binds a fixed token (`Make`, `SetOptions`, a grammar's `clear`,
+`Token(name, src)`, `Derive` and `Merge`), which is more than the merge
+repair that found it. Until then Go's merge gives TypeScript's result for
+every table Go can hold (`go/merge_fixed_test.go`); a randomized
+three-way differential of merges found no other split in it but the entry
+below and the instances only TypeScript's `empty()` builds
+(`go/doc/differences.md`, "Merge").
+
+Registered as `fixed-taken-earlier`, with `fixed-taken-later` as its
+control.
+
+### An instance with no fixed tokens left in Go
+
+**Deferred, not deliberate** — found by the merge differential, where a
+grammar `clear` on a merged instance reaches it, though no merge is
+needed.
+
+Go builds its text lexer's stop table from the fixed sources, and when
+there are none it falls back to the six default ones, `{}[]:,`
+(`buildLexTables`, `go/lexer.go`), a fallback meant for a hand-built
+lexer configuration. So an instance whose last fixed token went, every
+default removed or a grammar `clear`, ends text at each of the six and
+then has nothing to lex it with. TypeScript and Rust lex it as text.
+
+| options | `{` lexes as, TypeScript | Go | Rust |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `fixed.token`: five defaults `null`, `#CA` on `=` | `#TX` | `#TX` | `#TX` |
+| `fixed.token`: all six defaults `null` | `#TX` | **`unexpected`** | `#TX` |
+
+Repair direction: **Go changes**, applying the fallback only where there
+is no fixed-token table at all rather than where the table is empty. Not
+done with the merge repair, which leaves the lexer alone.
+
+Registered as `no-fixed-left`, with `one-fixed-left` as its control.
+
